# Aptitude Packet Manager

A simple command line tool to install linux (debian / ubuntu) software packages.

## Difference between apt and apt-get

*apt-get*:   A stable interface intended for scripting eg automatic parsing.

*apt*: More human friendly with nicer messages


## Searching

#### Search for an installed pakage

    apt list --installed  

    dpkg --get-selections | grep <name>

#### Search for an available packet:

    sudo apt-cache search <name>  

## Get more information about a packet

    apt show <packet-name>

## Get a list of all manually installed packages

    zcat -qf /var/log/apt/history.log* | grep " install "

## Installing 

#### Installation of a packet

    sudo apt install <packet>

#### Simulating withour installing:

Use option *-s* after apt-get or us *--dry-run* option

#### Removal of a packet

    sudo apt remove <packet>

After removal of a packages, some prerequistes are orpahn and should be removed:

    sudo apt autoremove

#### Installing Updates

    apt update       -> Updates the local list of packages in etc/apt/sources.list 
    apt upgrade      -> upgrades the system


## Add a new source to the Aptitude Package Manager

Use the following command:

    apt-add-repository ppa:<ppa-address>

The list of all sources is stored in /etc/apt/sources.list

